course: dictionary
lesson: 3838
title: A fortress is a strong, safe place
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A fortress is a large, strong building or town with thick walls, built to protect people from attack. «It remembers 14 July 1789, when people in Paris took the Bastille, a prison fortress.» The word «took» tells you the Bastille was a place people could capture, and «prison» tells you it held people inside. A fortress can be a strong place for defence and also a place for keeping prisoners.
explain: «Prince Dmytro Vyshnevetsky built a fortress on the island of Mala Khortytsia.» The clue is «built»: a fortress is something people construct on purpose, and it was made to be safe and hard to reach. You can check this by swapping in your own meaning: a strong building with thick walls still makes sense in that sentence.
explain: The word can also describe a place that feels very safe and hard to get into, even if it is not a building with walls. If someone says their room is a fortress, they mean nobody can easily get in. Look for clues like «thick walls», «guarded» or «locked» to tell which meaning the writer chose.
fact: A fortress is a large, strong building or town with thick walls, built to protect people from attack.
fact: «Fortress» can also mean any place that is very safe and hard to get into.
fact: In history lessons, fortresses include castles, walled towns and forts used in wars.
fact: In stories, a fortress is often the home of a king or a villain.
fact: The plural of fortress is fortresses.
exercise: «Prince Dmytro Vyshnevetsky built a fortress on the island of Mala Khortytsia.» What was built on the island? | A forest | A boat | *A fortress | A library
exercise: Sam says his bedroom, with its locked door and bolted window, is a fortress. What does he mean? | It is the biggest room in the house | It is full of old fortresses | *It is very safe and hard to get into | It is painted bright red
exercise: «It remembers 14 July 1789, when people in Paris took the Bastille, a prison fortress.» What is a fortress? | *A large, strong building with thick walls, built to protect people | A small shop that sells sweets | A song sung at night | A type of boat used on rivers
test: Which word is closest in meaning to fortress? | *Stronghold | Puddle | Garden | Market
test: The Bastille was a prison fortress in Paris. What was its main job? | Teaching children to read | Making music for the king | Growing crops for the town | *Holding prisoners in a strong building
exam: Which sentence uses fortress correctly? | The fortress rang the school bell at noon | The fortress was a bright colour of the sky | *The soldiers guarded the fortress day and night | She fortressed her homework to the bus stop
